  "worked_solution": "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where\n\n* $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n* $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe heater is a circular cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n* Since the distance from any point on the cylinder's surface to the $z$-axis equals $3,$ we must have $r=3$ for every point on the surface.\n\n* On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain, and $z$ varies arbitrarily in the domain $z\\in [0,3].$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r=3,$ where $(\\theta ,z)\\in [0,2\\pi )\\times [0,3].$",
